.TH CORPUS 1
.SH NAME
corpus \- an interned-corpus toolchain
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B corpus intern
[
.I file
]
.I wordlist widx corpus
.br
.B corpus print
.I wordlist widx corpus
.br
.B corpus hist
.I wordlist widx corpus
.br
.B corpus bigrams
.I wordlist widx corpus
.br
.B corpus mi
.I wordlist widx corpus
.br
.B corpus tscore
.I wordlist widx corpus
.br
.B corpus refs
.I wordlist widx corpus word
.br
.B corpus pconc
.I wordlist widx corpus context
.RB [ < "refs output" ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Corpus
is the second half of Kenneth Church's "Unix for Poets" tutorial, after
its own move from repeated string comparison to an
.I interned
representation: every distinct word in a text is assigned a small
integer once, and everything after that - counting, bigram generation,
significance scoring, concordancing - operates on those integers instead
of re-comparing strings. That is the part of the idea that is genuinely
portable; the mmap half of the original is not, since Limbo is a
garbage-collected virtual machine language with no route to raw pages,
and is not attempted here.
.PP
.B Intern
reads
.I file
(standard input if none given), splits it into words exactly as
.IR wordfreq (1)
does, and writes three files matching the original tutorial's own
formats:
.I wordlist
(the distinct words, NUL-delimited, in the order first seen),
.I wordlist.idx
(one big-endian 32-bit int per word: its byte offset in
.IR wordlist ),
and
.I corpus
(one big-endian 32-bit int per token of the input, in order: which word
it is).
.PP
.B Print
is the exact inverse of
.BR intern :
it reads
.I corpus
and writes the text back out, one word per line.
.PP
.B Hist
counts how often each word occurs - by indexing an array with the
corpus's own integers, not by sorting or hashing strings - and prints
each word with its count, most frequent first.
.B Bigrams
does the same for adjacent word pairs, encoding each pair as one integer
(id1 times the vocabulary size plus id2) and sorting those, the direct
integer analogue of
.IR uniq (1)'s
.BR -c .
.PP
.B Mi
and
.B tscore
print each bigram with a significance score, most significant first.
.B Mi
is pointwise mutual information,
.RI log2( N \(mu count( w1 , w2 )/(count( w1 )\(mu count( w2 ))),
which favours rare pairs that almost only ever occur together over
frequent ones - it is the score that finds a foreign phrase quoted once
in an English text, because the words in it cooccur nowhere else.
.B Tscore
is
.RI (count( w1 , w2 )\(mi count( w1 )\(mu count( w2 )/ N )/sqrt(count( w1 , w2 )),
which instead favours frequent, statistically dependable pairs. In both,
.I N
is the number of
.B tokens
in the corpus, not the number of distinct words - using the wrong one
scales every score by the same constant and the ranking still looks
plausible, which is exactly why it is spelled out here.
.PP
.B Refs
prints every position (a 0-based token index) at which
.I word
occurs, one per line.
.B Pconc
reads such a list of positions from standard input and prints, for
each, the
.I context
tokens either side of it - a keyword-in-context concordance, the same
pipeline shape as the tutorial's own
.B "refs <pattern> | pconc" .
.SH EXAMPLE
.TP
.EX
corpus intern genesis wordlist wordlist.idx corpus
corpus hist wordlist wordlist.idx corpus
corpus mi wordlist wordlist.idx corpus
corpus refs wordlist wordlist.idx corpus Pharaoh |
	corpus pconc wordlist wordlist.idx corpus 6
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/corpus.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR wordfreq (1),
.IR rev (1),
.IR paste (1)
.SH BUGS
.B Bigrams
counts by sorting the encoded pair integers - Church's own
.B count_by_sorting
- and not by the hash-table alternative he also shows,
.BR count_by_hashing ,
which is faster on a large corpus; not implemented, to keep this to one
working technique rather than two half-finished ones.
.B Refs
scans the whole corpus for every query rather than building and reusing
a persisted inverted index the way the tutorial's own
.B conc
file does; fine at the scale this was built and tested against, and the
first thing to change if a much larger corpus made repeated queries
slow.
